language js(typescript)

// R20 — an acronym keeps its capitals in a type name: UIStateStore, never
// UiStateStore. Half-capitalising one is what autocomplete produces, and it makes the
// same acronym read two ways in one tree — this repo had UiStateStore, DomScope,
// FpsMeter and ViewportHud beside an already-correct UV.
//
// The acronym may sit anywhere: at the front (UiState), at the back (ViewportHud), in
// the middle (ShapeUiPanel), or be the whole name (Ui). What marks it as a word rather
// than a coincidence is PascalCase itself — it either opens the name or follows a
// lowercase letter or digit, and it is either followed by a capital or ends the name.
// The three parts of the regex say exactly that, in order.
//
// The ESLint original wrote that with lookaheads. Grit's regex engine has none, so the
// same shape is expressed as an optional capital-initial prefix that must end lowercase,
// then the acronym, then an optional capital-initial suffix. It is equivalent because
// Grit matches the whole identifier: a name starting lowercase can satisfy no arm, which
// is what keeps `dogUrl` and `viewportHud` out, and a fully capitalised acronym never
// matches a half-capitalised alternative, which is why `UV`, `DOMScope` and `FPSMeter`
// stay silent without needing an exception.
//
// Do not write this as two alternatives anchored separately. Grit anchors a pattern by
// wrapping it as `^` + pattern + `$`, so a top-level `|` compiles to `^ARM1|ARM2$` —
// each arm anchored at one end only, and an acronym with capitals on both sides matched
// by neither. `ShapeUiPanel` went unreported for exactly that reason.
//
// `Rgba` is listed before `Rgb`, and `Uuid` alongside: the acronym has to match to its
// full length, so in `RgbaColor` the trailing `a` defeats the "followed by a capital"
// test and a list holding only `Rgb` reads as covering it while letting it through.
//
// Non-capturing groups are mandatory: a capture group makes the plugin fail to compile
// with "regex pattern matched 1 variables, but expected 0".
or {
    JsIdentifierBinding(),
    TsIdentifierBinding()
} as $identifier where {
    $identifier <: r"(?:[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*[a-z0-9])?(?:Ui|Ux|Fps|Hud|Dom|Uv|Fov|Url|Uri|Api|Css|Html|Xml|Json|Svg|Rgba|Rgb|Gpu|Cpu|Http|Uuid|Midi)(?:[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*)?",
    register_diagnostic(
        span = $identifier,
        message = "R20: an acronym keeps its capitals in a type name — UIStateStore, not UiStateStore. R13 then forces the filename to follow."
    )
}
